Here \(f(1)=1-2=-1\) and \(f(2)=4-2=2\). Linear interpolation gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
x &\approx 1-\frac{f(1)(2-1)}{f(2)-f(1)}\\
&= 1-\frac{(-1)(1)}{2-(-1)}\\
&= 1+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{4}{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus the first approximation is \(\frac{4}{3}\).

Newton's method gives
\[
x_1=x_0-\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}
\]
Here \(f(x)=x^2-3\), so \(f'(x)=2x\). With \(x_0=2\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_1 &= 2-\frac{2^2-3}{2(2)}\\
&= 2-\frac{1}{4}\\
&= \frac{7}{4}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus \(x_1=\frac{7}{4}\).

Newton's method gives
\[
x_1=x_0-\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}
\]
Here \(f(x)=x^3+4x^2-10\), so \(f'(x)=3x^2+8x\). Evaluate at \(x_0=1\), and thus compute \(x_1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
f(1) &= 1+4-10=-5\\
f'(1) &= 3+8=11\\
x_1 &= 1-\frac{-5}{11}=1+\frac{5}{11}=\frac{16}{11}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore option B is correct.
